okay for today's classic interview we're  going back to an awesome interview I did  with Michael Mann three and a half years  ago about the real impact of climate  change and I am completely sick of this  narrative that there is a debate as to  if and when climate change is going to  become a problem for humans on the  planet it's a problem now and Michael  Mann talked to us about that back in  December of 2015 what he said then is  particularly relevant now as everything  he talked about has accelerated and  there is no more sort of when is it that  we will be able to identify the  real-world effects of climate change  they're happening now Michael Mann knows  it our audience back in 2015 knew it and  we're going to go back to that interview  today with Michael Mann Michael Mann is  distinguished professor and director of  the Earth System science Center at Penn  State University and also author of two  books including dire predictions  understanding climate change and the  hockey stick and the climate Wars it's  so great to have you on and the book  really presents in sort of ordinary  straightforward language the important  findings of what is currently happening  with the the climate crisis on earth I  would love to start at the broadest  point possible with regard to what  predictions we can make and predictions  people may have heard about the future  of the climate crisis people sometimes  euro the entire East Coast will be  underwater or all of the ice will be  melted and the polar ice caps etc and  sometimes it sounds a little bit  hyperbolic so let's break it down what  are the real concerns with regards to  what we can predict will happen as a  result of continued climate change  Thanks it's good to be on the show with  you so if we continue with business as  usual burning of fossil fuels if we  don't take precautionary actions to deal  with our increased ongoing emissions of  carbon into the atmosphere we will  likely see between 7 &amp; 9 degrees  fahrenheit warming of the globe by the  end of the century now that won't you  know it  that doesn't mean that the the entire us  will be submerged below the ocean it  means that there will likely be enough  sea level rise to force us essentially  to retreat from many of the major  coastal cities of the US and elsewhere  around the world  it will mean that many of the problems  we are already seeing damaging impacts  of climate change-related increases in  extreme weather wildfires droughts  flooding all those things that we have  seen already worsened weather events  because of the impact of climate change  will get that much worse if we again  continue with business as usual burning  of fossil fuels by the end of the  century we warm the plant by seven to  nine degrees Fahrenheit arguably the  rates of change that we will see in our  climate will exceed our ability to adapt  to those changes  I'm curious Michael as far as the  temperature change you know we talked  about the sea level rise that we would  see what the if if our oceans were  warmer also would that not have a  significant impact on ocean life and  thus the entire animal ecosystem yeah  that's right in fact right now we have a  major El Nino event going on and it sort  of gives us a glimpse of the future  because during El Ninos often sea  surface temperatures or around the the  world are much warmer than they would  normally be and with those large El Nino  events like the one we're seeing right  now we often see a massive die-off of  corals we see an event called coral  bleaching where our corals they lose  their color because the symbiotic  organisms that give them that color  literally literally flee but the corals  can't move they're stuck and if that  goes on long enough that kills off the  corals that is combined with the effects  of ocean acidification as we increase  the o2 in the atmosphere the oceans  become more acidic so all of these  things are impacting ocean biota coral  reefs  this is part  the reason that climate change  represents a major threat to ocean  biodiversity to fish populations and our  ability to continue to feed ourselves  through fishing I would love to hear a  little bit from you about how our  audience can tackle when they are on the  receiving end of what to me sounds like  a tired old talking point from so called  climate sceptics or non non science  believers as I like to call them that  hey listen humans on earth we only have  observations about temperature from a  very tiny slice of the history of our  planet and there's really no way at all  to say that the the changes in  temperature we're seeing today are any  more than just cyclical temperature  changes we've had ice ages and it  logically follows we also have warmer  periods we only have a little bit of  data that's a very common talking point  we hear from climate sceptics what can  our audience have sort of in their  arsenal to refute that sure so I will  respond to that particular talking point  but it's often very useful to draw upon  resources that exist out there there is  a site called skeptical science calm and  if you go to that website they have a  list of the hundred or so leading  climate change denier talking points and  the scientific responses to each of  those talking points at the introductory  intermediate and advanced level that's  uh and even better than that you can  download the whole thing onto your  smartphone and have it at your  fingertips so you know what that  Thanksgiving dinner where that  cantankerous uncle that you have your  items aren't with you know you've got  that at your disposal and you can  respond to them in real time but with  that particular talking point it's  actually combining a number of myths  first of all it wouldn't even matter if  the current warmth that we've seen  whether or not it's unprecedented the  reason for past periods of warmth is  understood the reason that you know the  temperatures were so warm during the  early cretaceous period when dinosaurs  roamed the planet that was because of  very high co2 levels a very  greenhouse effect and that happened  naturally over tens of millions of years  due to natural variations in the  concentrations of these greenhouse gases  that have to do with things like plate  tectonics so those changes happen over a  timescale of you know it's a hundred  million years if you go back to the  early cretaceous period what we are  doing by burning fossil fuels is  literally taking all the carbon that got  buried in the ground after that period  over a hundred million years and we're  putting it back up into the atmosphere  but over a time scale of a hundred years  a million times faster so it's the rate  at which we are modifying the atmosphere  and warming the planet that is cause for  concern not whether or not there might  have been past periods periods in the  distant past there that were as warm one  other thing we do know that the recent  warming spike is unprecedented going  back into the last ice age and probably  back more than a hundred thousand years  there are various lines of paleo climate  evidence that we can use to piece  together the puzzle of how the climate  changed in the past and those data tell  us that the recent warming spike is  likely unprecedented in tens of  thousands of years maybe more than a  hundred thousand years so it is unusual  and the co2 concentrations that we've  reached already are higher than they've  been in millions of years we know that  as well and Michael how drastic do human  activities on earth have to change to  really make a dent in the impact that  we're having you know sometimes I'll  read about a new initiative for example  we could move 30% of New York State's  electricity consumption to renewable and  and green alternative energies on a per  capita basis and I'm thinking per capita  our population continues to increase  we're talking only about 30% it seems  like ten years from now any benefit that  we get from doing that is already going  to be undone by our population growth um  is my sort of pessimism or feeling  overwhelmed by the the magnitude of what  we have on our hands here justified or  can we really make a dent in this yeah I  think it's a little overly pessimistic  a population in the US isn't climbing  its population in developing nations  that continues to climb but when  demographers look at sort of the  large-scale trends right now with global  population they estimate that will  probably plateau by the middle of this  century at about nine million people so  yes there will be more people right now  there are about seven billion people but  that's not going to continue on an  exponential trajectory like it has in  the past it appears to be plateauing out  now can we provide energy for those  people without the continued burning of  fossil fuels that's the real challenge  and you know you mentioned you know the  possibility of New York generating 30  percent of its electricity from  renewables well Germany is already doing  it Germany is doing that the if you look  around the globe if you tally the total  global electricity use we are now up to  about 22 percent of our electricity  generation globally and coming from  renewables so there has been a dramatic  turning of the corner just over the past  few years in moving away from burning of  fossil fuels moving towards renewable  energy to generate electricity we've got  a lot farther to go but last year for  the first time ever for the first time  in many decades we saw global economic  growth without any growth in carbon  emissions right I mean there's a hard on  that that expensive way that that seems  to be a really important indicator of  where things could go with the right  decisions made is it not absolutely it  suggests that we're turning the corner  now we're not yet turning the corner  fast enough to stabilize those  greenhouse gas concentrations below  dangerous levels so it's going to take a  lot more work but there's reason for  cautious optimism we are already  beginning to turn the corner well any  conversation about climate change where  my view is unnecessarily pessimistic for  me is a win you know I mean that's  that's good news for sure  I'll remind our audience we've been  speaking with Michael Mann distinguished  professor and director of the Earth  System science Center at Penn State  University check out his book dire  predictions understand  climate change thanks so much for  joining me today thank you it was a  pleasure talking with you
 

